From: Artem Bambalov <artembambalov1993@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] gralloc: a flexible YUV buffer nobody reads is still a buffer

Opening a YouTube video in a browser took media.codec down every time,
and always by the same road:

  E BufferQueueProducer: [ImageReader-1x1f22u256m2-2297-0] dequeueBuffer:
      createGraphicBuffer failed
  E GraphicBufferAllocator: Failed to allocate (640 x 480) layerCount 1
      format 35 usage 100: 3
  E ACodec: Failed to allocate output port buffers after port
      reconfiguration: (-1010)
  E ACodec: Error occurred while disabling the output port
  E ACodec: signalError(omxError 0x80001001, internalError -110)
  F DEBUG: pid 318, omx@1.0-service >>> media.codec <<<, signal 11,
      fault addr 0x2c

Only the first line is anybody's fault. Everything under it is the
decoder, and then libnvomx, failing to survive an allocation that did
not happen.

The allocation is Chromium's. Its ImageReader takes each decoded frame
into a texture, and for that it asks for HAL_PIXEL_FORMAT_YCbCr_420_888
-- format 35, the flexible YUV -- with GRALLOC_USAGE_HW_TEXTURE and
nothing besides. gralloc.tegra.so refuses it, and the reason is written
plainly in the blob:

    34cc:  cmp   r5, #35   ; HAL_PIXEL_FORMAT_YCbCr_420_888
    34ce:  beq   3516
    3516:  tst.w fp, #255  ; usage & (SW_READ_MASK | SW_WRITE_MASK)
    351a:  beq   35bc      ; not one of them is set, so ...
    35bc:  mvn.w r0, #21   ; ... -EINVAL

NVIDIA reads 35 as "flexible YUV for the CPU" and will not hand one out
unless the caller means to touch it from software. The mirror rule sits
twenty instructions further on: format 0x22, the implementation-defined
one, is refused when software access *is* asked for. Between the two,
the blob has decided which format belongs to which consumer, and has
left no format at all for a YUV buffer that only ever gets sampled.

The platform disagrees, and it is the platform's contract. 35 is a
legitimate request for a texture-only buffer, which is why Chromium
makes it and why nothing else in the tree expects it to fail.

The blob is not wrong about what it can build, only about what it is
willing to be asked. So it is asked again, with the cheapest software
read bit set. It still chooses the layout from the hardware usage --
HW_TEXTURE gets block linear with or without the extra bit -- and it
strips the software bits back off before the allocation proper, so the
buffer that comes back is the one the caller described, not a different
one. The retry runs only after a refusal, and only for this one format
with no software usage at all; every other allocation takes the path it
always took.

Local playback never hit this: a SurfaceView asks for the opaque format
and sails through four resolution changes. It is the texture path, and
only the texture path, that has no format the blob accepts.

diff --git a/graphics/allocator/2.0/utils/passthrough/include/allocator-passthrough/2.0/Gralloc0Hal.h b/graphics/allocator/2.0/utils/passthrough/include/allocator-passthrough/2.0/Gralloc0Hal.h
--- a/graphics/allocator/2.0/utils/passthrough/include/allocator-passthrough/2.0/Gralloc0Hal.h
+++ b/graphics/allocator/2.0/utils/passthrough/include/allocator-passthrough/2.0/Gralloc0Hal.h
@@ -20,6 +20,7 @@
 #warning "Gralloc0Hal.h included without LOG_TAG"
 #endif
 
+#include <inttypes.h>
 #include <cstring>  // for strerror
 
 #include <allocator-hal/2.0/AllocatorHal.h>
@@ -132,8 +133,44 @@
 
         const native_handle_t* buffer = nullptr;
         int stride = 0;
+        uint64_t usage = info.usage;
         int result = mDevice->alloc(mDevice, info.width, info.height, static_cast<int>(info.format),
-                                    info.usage, &buffer, &stride);
+                                    usage, &buffer, &stride);
+
+        // NVIDIA's gralloc reads HAL_PIXEL_FORMAT_YCbCr_420_888 as "flexible YUV
+        // for the CPU", and turns the request down flat when the usage asks for no
+        // software access at all. In gralloc.tegra.so:
+        //
+        //     34cc:  cmp   r5, #35   ; HAL_PIXEL_FORMAT_YCbCr_420_888
+        //     34ce:  beq   3516
+        //     3516:  tst.w fp, #255  ; usage & (SW_READ_MASK | SW_WRITE_MASK)
+        //     351a:  beq   35bc      ; not one of them is set, so ...
+        //     35bc:  mvn.w r0, #21   ; ... -EINVAL
+        //
+        // The framework draws no such line. 35 is an ordinary thing to ask for
+        // when a buffer is only ever going to be sampled, and Chromium's
+        // ImageReader asks for exactly that: 640x480, format 35, usage
+        // GRALLOC_USAGE_HW_TEXTURE and nothing besides. The refusal arrives back
+        // up the stack as BAD_VALUE, the decoder cannot refill its output port
+        // after a reconfiguration (-1010), disabling that port then times out
+        // (-110), and libnvomx walks into a null pointer and takes media.codec
+        // down with it.
+        //
+        // So ask a second time with the cheapest software read bit set. The blob
+        // still picks the layout from the hardware usage -- HW_TEXTURE gets block
+        // linear either way -- and strips the software bits back off again before
+        // the allocation proper, so what comes back is the buffer the caller
+        // described.
+        if (result == -EINVAL && static_cast<int>(info.format) == HAL_PIXEL_FORMAT_YCbCr_420_888 &&
+            (usage & (GRALLOC_USAGE_SW_READ_MASK | GRALLOC_USAGE_SW_WRITE_MASK)) == 0) {
+            usage |= GRALLOC_USAGE_SW_READ_RARELY;
+            result = mDevice->alloc(mDevice, info.width, info.height,
+                                    static_cast<int>(info.format), usage, &buffer, &stride);
+            ALOGI("YCbCr_420_888 %ux%u refused for usage %" PRIx64
+                  ", asked again with a software read: %d",
+                  info.width, info.height, info.usage, result);
+        }
+
         switch (result) {
             case 0:
                 *outBuffer = buffer;
